# Frontend Engineering Standards

## Component Architecture
- Use functional components with explicit TypeScript interfaces for props. Never use `any`.
- Keep components small, focused, and single-purpose. Break complex views into modular subcomponents.
- Prefer controlled components for forms with strict schema validation (Zod / React Hook Form).
- Utilize early returns to avoid deeply nested JSX conditionals.

## Styling & Design System
- Adhere strictly to the project's design tokens (Tailwind CSS utility classes or design system tokens). Never introduce arbitrary hardcoded magic colors or raw hex values.
- Enforce responsive design mobile-first: ensure all interfaces render cleanly across mobile, tablet, and desktop breakpoints.
- Maintain consistent spacing rhythm and radius scale throughout the UI.

## Accessibility (a11y) & Usability
- Comply with WCAG 2.2 AA standards: ensure proper semantic HTML elements (`<main>`, `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<button>`).
- Provide accessible names (`aria-label`) for icon-only buttons.
- Ensure all interactive elements have visible focus rings (`focus-visible:ring-2`) and support full keyboard navigation.
- Never write text with insufficient color contrast against its background.

## State & Performance
- Keep state as local as possible. Do not lift state to global stores unless shared across distant routes.
- Implement proper loading skeletons, empty states, and Error Boundaries for all asynchronous data fetching.
- Optimize images using framework image components with explicit dimensions to eliminate Layout Shift (CLS).
